I happened to be looking at the code in question a couple of days ago.
NetHack actually throws away the bottom few bits of the PRNG it gets
from the system unless it's using an implementation that's known to be
good (and not have repetitive low-order bits, like many bad ones did).
There's also the option to use a NetHack-provided PRNG (one that used
to, and maybe still does, come with BSD, and which I've also observed
used by DJGPP, the library used for the DOS port), although it's off by
default.

Nowadays, public servers are increasingly using cryptographically secure
RNGs to prevent people being able to savescum via syncing up the seed
between the server and a local copy and savescumming the local copy,
repeating the keystrokes to exactly duplicate the game. "AdeonRNG",
created by the noted speedrunner, is on use in several public servers,
and also the /dev/null tournament; so I guess if you're getting bad luck
on /dev/null, blame him. NAO uses something custom that's more
efficient.

